o start with a clean art board  and to design from scratch that way it  allows that creative thinking to flow I  typically start with a desktop artboard  I know I know mobile first and all that  just go ahead and build and use a layout  grid I like setting up a twelve column  grid with a maximum grid width of 1440  pixels that's 1440 pixels  once your artboards and grids are set up  take a photo of your sketch upload it  and then put it on an extra artboard in  XD that makes it really easy for you to  reference it often step to block out  your sketch get a digital version of the  sketch as you drew it that way you'll  have all the components on the artboard  to move around later use shades of a  single color like gray or blue to keep  things simple and then use the same  symbols for images and videos that you  sketched indicate what content belongs  where using your headers estimate the  body copy length using placeholder text  and I use great xD plug-in called the  Florham Epsom by Pablo Claw chica that  makes this really really easy just I  just hit command option L and then click  the button and it fills that text space  once you're done  duplicate your artboard step 3 bring in  actual copy you want to start working  with actual copy actual text words even  if it's a first draft what so many  people don't realize is that writing  makes up so much of that positive user  experience so you want to bring in that  copy at a very very early phase trying  to move into visual design before  actually having copied is an uphill  battle I like to think of UX design as a  ping-pong match between our writer and a  designer there's so many of our other  visual things that we create are based  on the actual content the copy of the  page which is why you want to bring that  in at an early phase if you're a  designer and a writer UX design has a  very special place for you call me  working with actual copy can help avoid  bumps in the road later on like having  header that's too 